# Aliro reader component options (ESP-IDF). Applies to every app that pulls the
# aliro_reader component (the standalone reader and the Matter lock).

menu "Aliro reader"

config ALIRO_LAT_TRACE
	bool "Walk-up latency trace (aliro-lat budget line)"
	default y
	help
	  One-shot timestamps at every walk-up phase boundary (BLE connect ->
	  discovery -> L2CAP -> AUTH0/EXCHANGE -> M1-M4 -> first range ->
	  threshold -> bolt), printed as a single consolidated "aliro-lat"
	  budget line after the bolt is driven. Diagnostics only: one uptime
	  read + store per boundary, nothing on the UWB re-arm hot path.
	  Disable to compile the trace out entirely.

config WOZ_ALIRO_LAB
	bool "Aliro Lab transaction trace"
	default y
	depends on ALIRO_LAT_TRACE
	help
	  Compile in the structured "[ALAB] t=<us> ev=..." trace at transaction
	  phase boundaries so tools/aliro_lab.py can turn a captured serial log
	  into a scored walk-up report (phase waterfall, approach curve, checks).
	  Like the sibling `uwbdiag` trace, it ships in every Aliro build but
	  stays OFF at boot — the `lab on`/`lab off` console command toggles it
	  at runtime, off the UWB hot path. Its per-phase lines come from the
	  aliro-lat stamps, hence the ALIRO_LAT_TRACE dependency. Set n to strip
	  it entirely from a hardened production image.

config WOZ_RSSI_GATE
	bool "RSSI-gated UWB ranging (power gate)"
	default y
	help
	  Gate the UWB radio on BLE connection RSSI: the reader completes the
	  Aliro auth as soon as the phone connects, but holds the one message
	  the phone waits for before initiating ranging (Reader-Status-AP-
	  Completed, §11.7.3.4.1) until the smoothed RSSI says the phone is in
	  the last few metres. Until then the DW3000 stays powered off instead
	  of listening continuously from BLE range — the difference between a
	  battery-powered lock and a tethered one. A sustained fade below the
	  close threshold tears ranging down again and drops the link; the
	  phone re-runs the fast auth on its next approach. With this off the
	  radio arms right after auth, exactly as before.

config WOZ_RSSI_GATE_OPEN_DBM
	int "Gate open threshold (dBm)"
	depends on WOZ_RSSI_GATE
	default -55
	help
	  Smoothed connection RSSI at or above this opens the gate (allows UWB).

	  Set from a measured 274-sample curve (see docs/power-profile.md), not
	  from separation alone. Ranging needs about 1.5 s from AP-Completed to
	  its first trusted range, so the gate has to open roughly 2 m out or the
	  radio is still warming up when the phone reaches the door. On the bench
	  rig -55 lands in the 2.0-2.5 m band; -50 separates near from far better
	  on paper but opens under 1 m, which arrives too late to be useful.

config WOZ_RSSI_GATE_CLOSE_DBM
	int "Gate close threshold (dBm)"
	depends on WOZ_RSSI_GATE
	default -65
	help
	  Smoothed RSSI at or below this — sustained for the close hold time —
	  closes the gate: ranging stops, the DW3000 powers down and the link
	  is dropped. Keep well below the open threshold (hysteresis) so body
	  fades cannot flap the radio.

	  10 dB under the open threshold, which on the measured curve puts the
	  close edge past 3 m: you have to walk a metre back out before the radio
	  powers down. The old -75 was below the level seen at any distance the
	  rig could still hold a link at, so the gate effectively never closed.

config WOZ_RSSI_GATE_CLOSE_HOLD_MS
	int "Gate close hold (ms)"
	depends on WOZ_RSSI_GATE
	default 3000
	help
	  How long the smoothed RSSI must stay below the close threshold before
	  the gate closes. Rides out pocket/body fades mid-approach.

config WOZ_RSSI_GATE_MAX_HOLD_MS
	int "Longest AP-Completed hold (ms; 0 = unbounded)"
	depends on WOZ_RSSI_GATE
	default 0
	help
	  Cap on how long Reader-Status-AP-Completed may be deferred while the
	  gate stays closed. 0 holds indefinitely, which is the default, and the
	  bench is why.

	  The phone will not wait: measured patience is about 1.9 s, after which
	  it terminates the link (HCI reason 0x13) and reconnects. A capped hold
	  therefore trades that reconnect for arming UWB. Both arms were run.
	  Capping at 1200 ms released the gate at -75 and -72 dBm with the phone
	  still roughly 4 m out, and the DW3000 then listened to nothing for
	  2.8 s in one session and 6.6 s in the next. That is the exact waste the
	  gate exists to prevent, and it buys only the avoidance of a reconnect
	  that costs one fast re-auth, which is BLE-only and about 200 ms.

	  So: hold, let the phone give up, and let it come back when it is closer.
	  The unlock is not at risk, because an unprimed gate still fails open and
	  the phone demonstrably retries.

	  Set a nonzero value to take the other trade. If you do, note the cap is
	  only evaluated when an RSSI sample arrives, so the hold ends up to
	  WOZ_RSSI_GATE_POLL_MS late: a 1500 ms cap released at 1695 ms on the
	  bench, leaving 121 ms against the phone's 1873. Budget cap + poll.

config WOZ_RSSI_GATE_SLOPE_DB
	int "Fast-open rise (dB per 1.5 s window; 0 disables)"
	depends on WOZ_RSSI_GATE
	default 8
	help
	  A rise of this many dB across the tracking window opens the gate
	  early (once above the close threshold), so a fast walk-up is not
	  penalized by the smoothing lag. 0 turns the rise-rate fast open off
	  (threshold-only gating) — useful as the comparison arm in the power/
	  latency study.

config WOZ_RSSI_GATE_POLL_MS
	int "Connection RSSI poll period (ms)"
	depends on WOZ_RSSI_GATE
	default 250
	help
	  How often the transport reads the controller RSSI while the Aliro
	  L2CAP channel is up (one HCI Read RSSI per period; negligible next
	  to the UWB RX it replaces). The first read fires at channel-open so
	  a connect-at-the-door walk-up is never delayed by the poll interval.

config WOZ_ALIRO_STEPUP
	bool "Enable the Aliro step-up (Access Document) phase"
	default n
	help
	  Reference-completeness build of the Aliro v1.0 §8.4 step-up phase: after
	  the standard EXCHANGE, the reader can request an ISO 18013-5 (mdoc) Access
	  Document from the User Device (ENVELOPE / GET RESPONSE APDUs carrying a
	  session-encrypted DeviceRequest/DeviceResponse), then parse and verify it
	  per §7.4 on a background worker.

	  This is OFF by default and, when on, is armed one-shot from the console
	  (`aliro-stepup arm`); it never runs per-unlock. The verification verdict is
	  logged and stored only -- it NEVER gates the unlock, which the provisioned
	  trust store alone decides. With this option unset the reader is byte-for-byte
	  identical to today and the fast-path auth segment is untouched.

endmenu
